 \begin{abstract}
 % BEGIN-EV2
 A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. In a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, we show that for the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement the ban costs nothing, because replacement restores eligibility and the payoff-relevant future states, given honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a new identity while the budget allows and then keeps targeting the newest identities. A registered test compares it with a verified-history comparator, C$^*$, at matched budget and sanctions on six markets drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash.
-Four findings. First, pooled over the six markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ (C$^*$ minus R3 under D2+H3 attacks: \site{abs_q1_est}); an exploratory $t$ interval over the six market differences, [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}], does not establish the ordering beyond these markets, and the second attack family repeated the first family's winner in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} cells. Second, newest-first targeting does almost all the work: once it is in place, R3's priority window changes the attacker's gain by only \site{abs_q2_pg1}. Third, at the headline market the targeting also falls on an honest newcomer: R3 audits \site{abs_hon_r3_1} of a replacement identity's jobs in its second ten rounds, C$^*$ \site{abs_hon_cs_1}. Fourth, under D2+H3, R3 lowers the attacker's gain relative to uniform audits on every fresh market, but the registered replication passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six; at the headline market, welfare attacks lower all three rules' gross efficiency to about \site{abs_q3_w0}.
