 succeeded in 4ms:
+Four findings. First, pooled over the six markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ (C$^*$ minus R3 under D2+H3 attacks: \site{abs_q1_est}); exploratory $t$ intervals over the six market differences are [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}] with the frozen searches, [\site{abs_ml_dlo}, \site{abs_ml_dhi}] with the doubled searches, and [\site{abs_env_lo}, \site{abs_env_hi}] with each rule's strongest family, so the direction is fairly stable but the size, and with it the equivalence label, is fragile and not established beyond these markets; the second attack family repeated the first family's winner in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} cells. Second, newest-first targeting does most of the work: in R3's own comparison (with and without the priority window, under D2+H3), the window changes the attacker's gain by \site{abs_dp}, small next to its effect without newest-first targeting. Third, at the headline market the targeting also falls on an honest newcomer: R3 audits \site{abs_hon_r3_1} of a replacement identity's jobs in its second ten rounds, C$^*$ \site{abs_hon_cs_1} in the same bin, and the replacement's profit per round under R3 differs from that under C$^*$ by a fraction of \site{abs_hon_rel}. Fourth, under D2+H3, R3 lowers the attacker's gain relative to uniform audits on every fresh market, but the registered replication passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six; at the headline market, welfare attacks lower all three rules' gross efficiency to about \site{abs_q3_w0} while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 (gain \site{abs_q3_w0g}).
+\paragraph{The rule.} For B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation, immediate free replacement restores the same distribution of payoff-relevant future states up to identity relabelling, so a ban adds no continuation cost; refunds of failed jobs still apply (Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}). R3 gives audit priority to the jobs of identities in their first $L$ rounds, auditing all of them when the round's quota covers them, and spends half of the remaining audits on the newest established identities. Its priority window expires, but the newest-first targeting does not: while a replacement is uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least $\min(s, \lceil r/\const{2} \rceil)$ of the $r$ residual audits, $s$ being its served jobs (Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} and the remark after it). The lemma bounds the audits a replacement receives, not its deterrence, and the bound can be zero (ties, no served jobs, newer entrants, or a quota already spent). Age counts the rounds since an identity's first offer.
+\paragraph{Contributions.} Three findings lead. (i) On the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches classified R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent in attacker gain within the pre-set materiality margin of $\pm0.05$; the second Q1 family mostly repeats the first, and the classification is established for these six markets, not for the neighbourhood. (ii) In the measured factorial contrasts, R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the reduction in the attacker's gain, so given newest-first targeting the priority phase is close to redundant. (iii) In a registered diagnostic, R3 keeps auditing an honest provider's replacement identity well after its window, at a measured cost in that provider's profit per round: the targeting that works against the attacker also burdens honest newcomers. Supporting these are a conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless, measured failure boundaries (stronger and independently authored attackers, coalitions, the terminal-reserve collapse and a tested repair), and two registered tests whose original failure, amendments, and fresh-market replication are documented in a record to be released with the paper.
+\paragraph{Results in brief.} Q1: pooled over the six fresh markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{int_q1_cat}'' within the registered margin under X7-SC, and likewise under D2+H3 (the two families are close to one test, since X7-SC mostly selected the embedded D2+H3 winner): R3 is not materially better than the development-selected verified-history comparator at the same budget, and the difference varies in sign across markets. Q2: in the pooled factorial contrasts, the priority phase and newest-first targeting each lower the measured gain (main effects \site{int_q2_p} and \site{int_q2_g}), and either one alone accounts for most of the reduction. Q3: the training-selected attack envelopes have lower measured gains against R3 than against B3 on every evaluated market, a finite-search comparison that does not establish robustness to the searched families; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{int_q3_w0}, and exploratory lotteries over existing attacks, selected on the evaluation records, reach efficiency well below the \site{int_w1} of the registered W1 search at non-negative estimated gain, with public-family attacks alone; the W1 search therefore supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies, and the mixtures' population profitability is not established. Q4: the B3--R3 comparison passes on \site{int_q4_k} of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags; on the other three, R3's estimated advantage is positive but below the registered threshold.
+\paragraph{Verified-history targeting (second test).} The comparator family adapts credibility-based spot-checking and BOINC's adaptive replication to a fixed audit quota. An identity's credibility $p_i$ is its number of passed audits since its creation or its last failure. It is updated in a batch after each round's audits: if any audited job of the identity failed in that round, $p_i = 0$ for the next round, whatever its passes in the batch; otherwise $p_i$ grows by the round's passes. A replacement starts at zero, and audits selected in the current round are not passes. C-thr($K$) treats an identity as unproven while $p_i < K$ and audits unproven identities first, in ascending $p_i$; it then selects $\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual jobs by ascending $p_i$ plus the picks already assigned to that identity in the round, and the rest uniformly. C-mem($K$, $M$) counts only the passes of the last $M$ rounds, so scrutiny returns to identities that are not audited. C-taper($K$) and C-boinc have no priority phase: they audit by weighted sampling without replacement, with weight $K/(p_i + K)$ (C-taper) or $1$ if $p_i < 10$ and $1/p_i$ otherwise (C-boinc, after BOINC's trust threshold of 10~\cite{boincwiki}); the weights are relative selection weights, not audit probabilities. Twelve configurations were compared in development at the fixed pair and R3's pacing, by the development tuning objective: the minimum, over the headline market, a development market, and the attack families, of the smaller of a gross-efficiency margin and an attacker-gain margin. The efficiency margin was the smaller at every configuration, so the selection was in effect on gross efficiency, and at the inner search budget every configuration's selected attack had zero or negative gain (Table~\ref{tab:comparator-dev}). The two best were rechecked at full budget; the selected comparator is C$^* = $ C-mem($K = \const{6}$, $M = \const{20}$), and the runner-up is C-thr($K = \const{12}$). Both full-budget scores were decided by D2 at dev0 (\const{0.103} against \const{0.098}), while under D2+H3 the runner-up left the lower gain (\const{0.076} against \const{0.097} at the headline market, \const{0.018} against \const{0.038} at dev0). This is a two-stage screen: it selects the winner among the two finalists and does not rank all twelve configurations at full budget. C$^*$ is the winner of this screen under the development objective; it is not the strongest possible credibility rule, and R3 embodies earlier development choices that the comparison does not equalise. C$^*$/S0 is C$^*$ with B3's spread pacing.
+\paragraph{Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1).} Pooled over the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C$^*$ higher than against R3 by \site{r_q1_d_est} under D2+H3 and by \site{r_q1_x_est} under X7-SC; the mean gains are \site{r_abs_cs} against C$^*$ and \site{r_abs_r3} against R3 under D2+H3, and \site{r_abs_cs_x} and \site{r_abs_r3_x} under X7-SC. Both direction tests reject $d = \const{0}$ (calibrated input $p' = $ \site{r_q1_d_p}; Holm-adjusted $p = $ \site{r_q1_holm} for each). Both intervals lie inside the registered margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: the categories are ``\site{r_q1_d_cat}'' (D2+H3) and ``\site{r_q1_x_cat}'' (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is ``\site{r_q1_d_eff}''. Under the registered margin, R3 is not materially better than the development-selected comparator; the margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ is half the first test's comparative margin, not an estimated threshold of buyer indifference, and a narrower margin could order the two rules. The difference changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from \site{r_q1_f3_d} on fresh3 to \site{r_q1_f1_d} on fresh1 under D2+H3. Search uncertainty matters at this level: evaluating the doubled-search winners on the same seeds against the same frozen references (Table~\ref{tab:ph-doubled}, exploratory) changes fresh3's difference from \site{r_dbl_f3} to \site{r_dbl_f3_d}, and the pooled differences to \site{r_dbl_pool_d} under D2+H3 and \site{r_dbl_pool_x} under X7-SC; no decision is recomputed. The registered intervals measure seed uncertainty in six fixed markets. Treating the six markets as a sample of the neighbourhood (exploratory), a $t$ interval over the per-market differences is [\site{r_ml_lo}, \site{r_ml_hi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_pos} of six markets positive, and [\site{r_ml_xlo}, \site{r_ml_xhi}] under X7-SC; with the doubled-search winners it is [\site{r_ml_dlo}, \site{r_ml_dhi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_dpos} positive. With each rule's strongest training-selected family the interval is [\site{r_env_lo}, \site{r_env_hi}], with \site{r_env_pos} of six markets positive. The direction is therefore fairly stable across these analyses, while the size is not: every upper end is near the margin, so the fragile part is the equivalence label, not the sign. The frozen D2+H3 interval contains zero and crosses the margin, so this analysis does not establish the category for the neighbourhood; the X7-SC interval excludes zero and lies inside the margin, but that family mostly repeats D2+H3 (below). In relative terms, under D2+H3, the attacker's pooled mean gain against C$^*$ exceeds that against R3 by a fraction of \site{r_rel}. The two Q1 families are close to one test: X7-SC's search starts from the embedded D2+H3 winner, and in \site{r_x7_same} of the \site{r_x7_cells} Q1 cells it selected that winner, with identical evaluated gains; only fresh3 against C$^*$ and fresh4 against R3 differ. With the headline market, the same holds in \site{r_x7_all} of \site{r_x7_all_n} market--rule cells, which is why most of prediction Q3-a's differences are exactly zero. With each rule's strongest training-selected family instead (Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope}), the pooled difference is \site{r_env_pool}, and \site{r_env_f1} on fresh1, where X5-AG/v2 is C$^*$'s strongest family. C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain than C$^*$ (Section~\ref{sec:rules}); a comparator selected on attacker gain could change the direction label. The two rules differ more in whom they burden than in the attacker's gain. In the registered descriptive honest-replacement diagnostic at the headline market (Table~\ref{tab:ph-honest}), an honest provider replaces its identity at round \const{50}: R3 audits \site{r_hon_r3_0} and \site{r_hon_r3_1} of the replacement's served jobs in its first two ten-round age bins, C$^*$ audits \site{r_hon_cs_0} and \site{r_hon_cs_1}, and B3 about \site{r_hon_b3_0}. Under R3 the replacement earns less per round (relative to C$^*$, a fraction of \site{r_hon_rel}; the registered diagnostic stores bin means, so no interval is available) and loses more receipts to refunds paid to buyers on false audit failures; R3 directs more of the fixed audit capacity to this provider, which does not show higher total audit spending. Both decisions are about the prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test uses them through its references (addendum); their nominal error guarantees are not established on the fresh, selected-rule distributions (Section~\ref{sec:design}). The paired descriptive differences in Table~\ref{tab:q1-side} are small for efficiency, audit spending, refunds on false audit failures, and the market service fraction; the reference-profit column is in raw profit units. Splitting $d$ with the same normalisation into the attacked-profit term and the reference-profit term (Table~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, exploratory) shows that almost all of it is attacked profit: the attacker earns more under C$^*$ when it deviates, not when it is truthful.
+\caption{Q1: pooled intervals of $d$ against the equivalence and materiality margins. These registered intervals hold the six markets fixed; the exploratory market-level intervals are in the text.}\label{fig:q1}
+\paragraph{Which parts of R3 matter (Q2).} Under separately searched D2+H3 attacks, pooled over the fresh markets, the main effects on the gain are \site{r_q2_p} for the priority phase P and \site{r_q2_g} for newest-first residual targeting G, both reductions, and \site{r_q2_s} for pacing S, a small increase (X7-SC: \site{r_q2_s_x}). The two targeting parts are substitutes: the PG factorial contrast, half the difference between G's conditional effects, is \site{r_q2_pg}; G changes the gain by \site{r_q2_g_p0} without the priority phase and by only \site{r_q2_g_p1} with it, and the priority phase changes it by only \site{r_q2_p_g1} once G is in place (averaged over both pacing settings). R3's own comparison, $F_{111}$ minus $F_{011}$ (exploratory, from the evaluation records), is \site{r_dp} pooled: exactly zero on four markets, where both rules leave the attacker the same gain, and nonzero on fresh1 (\site{r_dp_f1}, larger than the doubling threshold, so the priority phase matters there) and fresh3. Given newest-first targeting the priority phase adds little at these audit ratios and capacities; at a lower audit ratio or a larger attacker capacity, half of a round's audits may no longer cover one provider's jobs, and the substitution need not hold. In the pooled contrasts, either P or G alone accounts for most of the measured reduction; adding the other contributes little. The pattern also appears in the fixed-attack contrasts averaged over the eight selected attacks (P: \site{r_q2_fixed_p}). Our registered conjectures (P negligible, the targeting parts additive, S helpful) failed as scored (Table~\ref{tab:predictions}); the stricter eligibility reading of Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} leaves them not assessed, with the estimates unchanged. X7-SC agrees (Table~\ref{tab:q2-x7sc}).
+\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope: the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. These are finite-search results, not bounds on the families. Against B3 the selected envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it is \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. H4 was written in a separate session against the frozen defenders and committed by its hash before the lock; its author's containment note states that it contains D2+H3 exactly as a special case, so it extends the public family rather than offering a disjoint attack concept. Evaluated at the headline market only, its selected search gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and so is R3's robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness. At the headline market, the selected W0 attacks (no profit constraint) yield gross efficiency \site{r_q3_w0_f000} for B3 and \site{r_q3_w0} for R3, with gains relative to the truthful reference of \site{r_q3_w0g_f000} and \site{r_q3_w0g_f111}; C$^*$ is like R3. Under the prescribed W1 searches (mean gain at least zero), the selected feasible attacks yield efficiency \site{r_q3_w1} for R3, while B3 stays at \site{r_q3_w1_f000} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). R3's selected W1 policy has an estimated gain of exactly zero, and the doubled W1 searches for R3 and C$^*$ select truthful delivery (zero shading), with the option to replace after a failed audit left on: the W1 search found no profitable welfare attack against them. These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class. An episode-level mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode, has the probability-weighted mean of its components' gains and efficiencies, so it satisfies a mean-gain constraint only when those weighted gains do; the following rows are that arithmetic on the existing evaluation records. With the H4 attack as companion, at W0 weights selected on the evaluation records, the mixtures reach gross efficiencies of \site{r_ph_e_r3} for R3 and \site{r_ph_e_cs} for C$^*$ at non-negative estimated gain; with public D2+H3 companions, R3's mixture (\site{r_ph_e_r3_d}) meets the registered training constraint, and C$^*$'s does so at the smaller weight \site{r_ph_fw_cs}, with efficiency \site{r_ph_fe_cs} against \site{r_ph_w1_cs} under the registered W1 search (Tables~\ref{tab:ph-welfare} and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens}). Weights and companions were chosen on the evaluation seeds with an unadjusted nominal one-sided \const{95} percent bound, so these rows show that the prescribed W1 search did not consider episode-level mixtures that reach lower empirical efficiency; they are not out-of-sample estimates, and population profitability remains unestablished. The mixture efficiencies stay above \const{0.90}; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised members, the selected total gains are \site{r_q3_coal_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c2} against R3 (search flagged); for five, \site{r_q3_coal_f000_c5} and \site{r_q3_coal_f111} (total objective), while the policy selected for the minimum-member objective leaves R3's worst member a mean gain of \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c5min}; the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives \site{r_q3_db_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_db_f111} against R3. These comparisons establish neither coalition stability nor an ordering across every coalition family (Table~\ref{tab:q3-coalitions}). At $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves \site{r_rep_f111} under D2+H3; the repair R4 lowers this to \site{r_rep_r4} and C$^*$ with the capped schedule (C$^*$/S2) to \site{r_rep_cs2} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-repair}).
+\paragraph{Search quality.} No cell is missing. \site{r_flag} of the \site{r_flag_n} doubling checks are flagged, and fresh-market factorial cells outside Q1 and Q4 have no doubling check, so their narrow intervals describe seed noise only. A nesting test on the existing records (exploratory) shows that unflagged cells can also be under-searched: X7-SC contains D2+H3, yet in \site{r_nest_over} of \site{r_nest_n} cells the X7-SC winner's evaluated gain falls short of the better of the frozen and doubled D2+H3 winners on the same seeds by more than the doubling threshold (at most \site{r_nest_max}). The report lists the search-flagged cells, and the addendum traces the inherited flags of every Q1 and Q4 decision through the records' references. Q2 and Q3 statements are descriptive; the cells they use carry the flags listed in the report. Table~\ref{tab:ph-stability} sets the Q1 estimates beside their re-evaluation with the registered configurations, and Table~\ref{tab:ph-q4-stability} compares the Q4 estimates, calibrated lower bounds, and decisions under both configurations and gives the adjusted market $p$-value under the registered configuration; the largest Q4 change is on fresh4, where the gain difference moves from \site{r_st_f4_old} to \site{r_st_f4_new}, still above the registered \const{0.10}.
+For the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin in the six sampled markets under both Q1 families, which are close to one test; across markets the difference varies in sign, and this analysis does not establish the ordering for the neighbourhood. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about \site{c_w0} against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$. The registered W1 search found no profitable welfare attack (gross efficiency near \site{c_w1}); exploratory mixtures of a welfare attack and a profitable attack reach lower efficiency at non-negative estimated gain, so it supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies. These conclusions concern frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.

