docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:6:\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}\newtheorem{corollary}{Corollary}\newtheorem{proposition}{Proposition}
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:12:\title{Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free:\\Two Registered Tests of an Audit Rule}
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:20:A buyer of computation can check only a sample of delivered jobs, and a sanctioned provider can return at once under a new identity. In a repeated procurement market with noisy delivery, costly exact audits, refunds, finite bans, and free identity replacement, we show that for the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement the ban costs nothing, because replacement restores eligibility and the payoff-relevant future states, given honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity. The rule we study, R3, audits every job of a new identity while the budget allows and then keeps targeting the newest identities. A registered test compares it with a verified-history comparator, C$^*$, at matched budget and sanctions on six markets drawn after the lock from a Bitcoin block hash.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:21:Four findings. First, pooled over the six markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{abs_q1_cat}'' within a pre-set margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ (C$^*$ minus R3 under D2+H3 attacks: \site{abs_q1_est}); exploratory $t$ intervals over the six market differences are [\site{abs_ml_lo}, \site{abs_ml_hi}] with the frozen searches, [\site{abs_ml_dlo}, \site{abs_ml_dhi}] with the doubled searches, and [\site{abs_env_lo}, \site{abs_env_hi}] with each rule's strongest family, so the direction is fairly stable but the size, and with it the equivalence label, is fragile and not established beyond these markets; the second attack family repeated the first family's winner in \site{abs_x7_same} of \site{abs_x7_cells} cells. Second, newest-first targeting does most of the work: in R3's own comparison (with and without the priority window, under D2+H3), the window changes the attacker's gain by \site{abs_dp}, small next to its effect without newest-first targeting. Third, at the headline market the targeting also falls on an honest newcomer: R3 audits \site{abs_hon_r3_1} of a replacement identity's jobs in its second ten rounds, C$^*$ \site{abs_hon_cs_1} in the same bin, and the replacement's profit per round under R3 differs from that under C$^*$ by a fraction of \site{abs_hon_rel}. Fourth, under D2+H3, R3 lowers the attacker's gain relative to uniform audits on every fresh market, but the registered replication passes on \site{abs_q4_k} of six; at the headline market, welfare attacks lower all three rules' gross efficiency to about \site{abs_q3_w0} while earning less than the truthful reference against R3 (gain \site{abs_q3_w0g}).
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:22:The claims hold for the prescribed searches and frozen references, the evaluated markets, fixed parameters, the searched families, and honest opponents that never replace their identities, so honest churn is untested; C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:30:We study what checking alone can do when the usual remedies against cheap identities are unavailable. The mechanism class excludes entry fees, identity certification, and collateral before receipts, and a sanctioned provider may re-enter at once under a fresh identity. The rules refund the escrow of a failed job and exclude the serving identity for a finite number of rounds. The population is a fixed set of providers, each with one active identity at a time; concurrent aliases, pre-aged reserve identities, and additional entrants (Sybil attacks~\cite{douceur2002sybil}) are outside the model.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:32:\paragraph{Checking newcomers is an old idea.} Intensive verification until an identity has earned credibility is established: credibility-based spot-checking analyses rejoining under new identities~\cite{sarmenta2001ccgrid,sarmenta2001sabotage}; BOINC offers an optional adaptive-replication policy that replicates a host's jobs until the host passes a trust threshold of consecutive valid results~\cite{anderson2009diversity,boincwiki}; customs guidance recommends checking all declarations of new importers for a period~\cite{walsh2003postrelease}. Checking can also depend on what a participant reports~\cite{zarkoob2020spot}; our admissible rules may read offers and claims, but we study targeting by identity age and verified history. The question we ask is narrower: under a hard audit budget, does R3's persistent age-ranked targeting do better or worse than targeting by verified history, when everything else is held equal?
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:34:\paragraph{The rule.} For B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity and with re-entry before the next allocation, immediate free replacement restores the same distribution of payoff-relevant future states up to identity relabelling, so a ban adds no continuation cost; refunds of failed jobs still apply (Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}). R3 gives audit priority to the jobs of identities in their first $L$ rounds, auditing all of them when the round's quota covers them, and spends half of the remaining audits on the newest established identities. Its priority window expires, but the newest-first targeting does not: while a replacement is uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least $\min(s, \lceil r/\const{2} \rceil)$ of the $r$ residual audits, $s$ being its served jobs (Lemma~\ref{lem:coverage} and the remark after it). The lemma bounds the audits a replacement receives, not its deterrence, and the bound can be zero (ties, no served jobs, newer entrants, or a quota already spent). Age counts the rounds since an identity's first offer.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:40:\paragraph{Results in brief.} Q1: pooled over the six fresh markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{int_q1_cat}'' within the registered margin under X7-SC, and likewise under D2+H3 (the two families are close to one test, since X7-SC mostly selected the embedded D2+H3 winner): R3 is not materially better than the development-selected verified-history comparator at the same budget, and the difference varies in sign across markets. Q2: in the pooled factorial contrasts, the priority phase and newest-first targeting each lower the measured gain (main effects \site{int_q2_p} and \site{int_q2_g}), and either one alone accounts for most of the reduction. Q3: the training-selected attack envelopes have lower measured gains against R3 than against B3 on every evaluated market, a finite-search comparison that does not establish robustness to the searched families; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{int_q3_w0}, and exploratory lotteries over existing attacks, selected on the evaluation records, reach efficiency well below the \site{int_w1} of the registered W1 search at non-negative estimated gain, with public-family attacks alone; the W1 search therefore supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies, and the mixtures' population profitability is not established. Q4: the B3--R3 comparison passes on \site{int_q4_k} of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags; on the other three, R3's estimated advantage is positive but below the registered threshold.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:44:\paragraph{Verification-based probation.} Credibility-based spot-checking accepts results according to a worker's verified history and analyses rejoining under new identities~\cite{sarmenta2001ccgrid,sarmenta2001sabotage}; follow-on work treats imperfect checks~\cite{watanabe2010generalized}. BOINC's adaptive replication keeps a consecutive-valid count per host and trusts a host above a threshold~\cite{anderson2009diversity,boincwiki}. CrowdEval screens workers with gold tasks before balancing gold and peer evaluation~\cite{qiu2018crowdeval}. Customs guidance recommends checking all declarations of new importers for a period~\cite{walsh2003postrelease}, and a field experiment found no overall compliance gain from close supervision of new firms~\cite{gangl2014supervision}. In a model with adverse selection and moral hazard, optimal long-term contracts screen and then put high types on probation with monitoring that declines with the track record~\cite{bhaskar2025screening}. Our comparator family adapts the credibility and BOINC rules to a fixed audit quota (Section~\ref{sec:rules}).
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:46:\paragraph{Cheap pseudonyms.} With cheap pseudonyms, unfavourable treatment of newcomers is the price of cooperation~\cite{friedman2001social}; whitewashing degrades peer-to-peer systems when turnover is high~\cite{feldman2006free}; without a central authority one attacker can create many identities~\cite{douceur2002sybil}. Reputation mechanisms with pure moral hazard and the cost of changing identities are analysed in~\cite{dellarocas2005reputation}. Reputation without persistent identity in markets of AI agents, with a one-time cash-out deviation, is studied in~\cite{gatta2026tempting}; that model has no verification budget.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:48:\paragraph{Costly verification and audits.} Costly state verification goes back to~\cite{townsend1979optimal}; history-dependent audit groups under restricted penalties go back to~\cite{harrington1988enforcement}; allocation with costly verification~\cite{benporath2014optimal}, limited punishments~\cite{li2020mechanism}, financially constrained agents~\cite{li2021financially}, and ex post verification with limited penalties~\cite{mylovanov2017optimal} assume persistent agents. Recursive inspection games spend a finite stock of inspections~\cite{vonstengel2016recursive}; audit games optimise randomised inspection against a best-responding attacker~\cite{blocki2013audit}; targeted and untargeted inspection under fixed capacity~\cite{liu2013enforcement} and dynamic monitoring under resource constraints~\cite{solan2021dynamic} are also studied; optimal committed audit policies against the worst equilibrium of the audited agents, with budgeted variants, are derived in~\cite{das2026optimally}. Tournament-based dynamic targeting allocates a fixed inspection capacity by relative compliance rank and compares it with standards-based and random inspection~\cite{gilpatric2015competition}; R3 ranks by identity age instead. Our rules are searched and tuned heuristics, not optima of these models. Paid computation with verification by recomputation and colluding workers is analysed in~\cite{fernandezanta2015algorithmic}, and cheating in outsourced distributed computation in~\cite{golle2001uncheatable}.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:63:\paragraph{Identities.} Each provider acts through a current identity. In any round it may retire the identity and re-enter under a fresh one at entry cost $e \ge 0$ ($e = 0$ in both tests). Rules see identities only as opaque tokens, must be invariant to relabelling them, and cannot link a fresh identity to its provider. An identity's age is the number of rounds since its first offer. Every identity is new at the start. The honest opponents never replace their identities, so after the start a new identity belongs to a provider that has replaced, usually the focal one; a truthful focal provider may also replace after a false failure, so a new identity marks a replacement history, not dishonest delivery. Population churn among honest opponents is untested, except for the single replacement of the second test's honest-replacement diagnostic (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}).
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:70:\paragraph{Baselines.} B2 posts prices for three claim tiers, allocates by those prices, and audits uniformly. B3 allocates each job, in descending order of value, to the offer with remaining capacity and the highest nonnegative score $v_j c_i - \mathrm{bid}_i$, pays $v_j c_i - s_{2,j}$ with $s_{2,j}$ the highest score among the other eligible offers (zero if there is none), and audits uniformly at random among served jobs, spread over the rounds. Both refund a failed job and ban its identity for $L$ rounds.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:72:\paragraph{R3.} R3 keeps B3's allocation, payment, tolerance, and enforcement and changes only which jobs it audits and when. \emph{Pacing:} with the integer allowance $A = \lfloor B/\alpha \rfloor$, R3 reserves $\min(JL, A)$ audits for the last $L$ rounds, in which it audits every served job while audits remain, and spends the rest evenly over the earlier rounds, carrying unspent quota forward. \emph{Selection:} it first audits the served jobs of identities younger than $L$ rounds, newest first; it then spends $\lceil r/2 \rceil$ of the $r$ remaining audits on the newest established identities and the rest uniformly at random.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:74:\paragraph{The factorial (second test).} The eight factorial rules and the comparators share B3's allocation and payment, refund-and-ban enforcement, no bond, the allowance $A = \lfloor B/\alpha \rfloor$, and the fixed pair $\tau = \const{0.16}$, $L = \const{10}$, which the registered tuning of B3 and of R3 had each chosen. They differ in three parts: a priority phase P (served jobs of identities younger than $L$ first, newest first), newest-first residual targeting G ($\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual audits to the newest identities), and pacing S (R3's terminal reserve and even early quota, against B3's spread schedule). The rules $F_{PGS}$ include $F_{000} = $ B3 and $F_{111} = $ R3. B2 keeps its posted prices and serves only as a reference at the headline market.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:76:\paragraph{Verified-history targeting (second test).} The comparator family adapts credibility-based spot-checking and BOINC's adaptive replication to a fixed audit quota. An identity's credibility $p_i$ is its number of passed audits since its creation or its last failure. It is updated in a batch after each round's audits: if any audited job of the identity failed in that round, $p_i = 0$ for the next round, whatever its passes in the batch; otherwise $p_i$ grows by the round's passes. A replacement starts at zero, and audits selected in the current round are not passes. C-thr($K$) treats an identity as unproven while $p_i < K$ and audits unproven identities first, in ascending $p_i$; it then selects $\lceil r/2 \rceil$ residual jobs by ascending $p_i$ plus the picks already assigned to that identity in the round, and the rest uniformly. C-mem($K$, $M$) counts only the passes of the last $M$ rounds, so scrutiny returns to identities that are not audited. C-taper($K$) and C-boinc have no priority phase: they audit by weighted sampling without replacement, with weight $K/(p_i + K)$ (C-taper) or $1$ if $p_i < 10$ and $1/p_i$ otherwise (C-boinc, after BOINC's trust threshold of 10~\cite{boincwiki}); the weights are relative selection weights, not audit probabilities. Twelve configurations were compared in development at the fixed pair and R3's pacing, by the development tuning objective: the minimum, over the headline market, a development market, and the attack families, of the smaller of a gross-efficiency margin and an attacker-gain margin. The efficiency margin was the smaller at every configuration, so the selection was in effect on gross efficiency, and at the inner search budget every configuration's selected attack had zero or negative gain (Table~\ref{tab:comparator-dev}). The two best were rechecked at full budget; the selected comparator is C$^* = $ C-mem($K = \const{6}$, $M = \const{20}$), and the runner-up is C-thr($K = \const{12}$). Both full-budget scores were decided by D2 at dev0 (\const{0.103} against \const{0.098}), while under D2+H3 the runner-up left the lower gain (\const{0.076} against \const{0.097} at the headline market, \const{0.018} against \const{0.038} at dev0). This is a two-stage screen: it selects the winner among the two finalists and does not rank all twelve configurations at full budget. C$^*$ is the winner of this screen under the development objective; it is not the strongest possible credibility rule, and R3 embodies earlier development choices that the comparison does not equalise. C$^*$/S0 is C$^*$ with B3's spread pacing.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:88:\begin{corollary}[uniform auditing under replacement]\label{cor:uniform}
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:90:\end{corollary}
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:93:R3 audits every served job of identities younger than $L$ rounds (age counted from the first offer) whenever the round's audit count covers them. Under overload, jobs are audited by age group, newest first, with $k$ the round's available audit count; an identity that offers but wins no jobs still ages; within the focal identity's group of $n$ jobs, of which $s$ are its own, the number audited is hypergeometric with $k' = \min\{n, \max(0, k - H)\}$ draws, $H$ being the fresh jobs of strictly newer identities.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:112:Every unilateral attacker family reads only the provider's own history across its identities, the round, its own parameters, and the rule's public constants $T$ and $L$; it does not observe the remaining global budget or other identities' ranks. Coalition members may share their members' own histories. For each parametrised family a registered search (CMA-ES with restarts on training seeds) finds the attack. A rule's truthful reference is then selected on training seeds from one menu built from its D2+H3 and X7-SC searches and frozen; the W1 welfare searches, which need that reference, run after the freeze; coalitions use their own staged joint truthful reference; and no evaluation-seed episode of a market runs before all of its choices are frozen.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:169:\paragraph{Fresh markets.} The block hash seeds a generator of market instances around the headline market: fixed scale, noise, budget ratio, audit cost, and cost-quality association, with provider ceilings, costs, capacities, and the top job value drawn per instance (Appendix~\ref{app:params}). Six instances and 200 evaluation seeds per market were drawn from it. The claims concern this neighbourhood, not markets in general.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:191:\paragraph{Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1).} Pooled over the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C$^*$ higher than against R3 by \site{r_q1_d_est} under D2+H3 and by \site{r_q1_x_est} under X7-SC; the mean gains are \site{r_abs_cs} against C$^*$ and \site{r_abs_r3} against R3 under D2+H3, and \site{r_abs_cs_x} and \site{r_abs_r3_x} under X7-SC. Both direction tests reject $d = \const{0}$ (calibrated input $p' = $ \site{r_q1_d_p}; Holm-adjusted $p = $ \site{r_q1_holm} for each). Both intervals lie inside the registered margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: the categories are ``\site{r_q1_d_cat}'' (D2+H3) and ``\site{r_q1_x_cat}'' (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is ``\site{r_q1_d_eff}''. Under the registered margin, R3 is not materially better than the development-selected comparator; the margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ is half the first test's comparative margin, not an estimated threshold of buyer indifference, and a narrower margin could order the two rules. The difference changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from \site{r_q1_f3_d} on fresh3 to \site{r_q1_f1_d} on fresh1 under D2+H3. Search uncertainty matters at this level: evaluating the doubled-search winners on the same seeds against the same frozen references (Table~\ref{tab:ph-doubled}, exploratory) changes fresh3's difference from \site{r_dbl_f3} to \site{r_dbl_f3_d}, and the pooled differences to \site{r_dbl_pool_d} under D2+H3 and \site{r_dbl_pool_x} under X7-SC; no decision is recomputed. The registered intervals measure seed uncertainty in six fixed markets. Treating the six markets as a sample of the neighbourhood (exploratory), a $t$ interval over the per-market differences is [\site{r_ml_lo}, \site{r_ml_hi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_pos} of six markets positive, and [\site{r_ml_xlo}, \site{r_ml_xhi}] under X7-SC; with the doubled-search winners it is [\site{r_ml_dlo}, \site{r_ml_dhi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_dpos} positive. With each rule's strongest training-selected family the interval is [\site{r_env_lo}, \site{r_env_hi}], with \site{r_env_pos} of six markets positive. The direction is therefore fairly stable across these analyses, while the size is not: every upper end is near the margin, so the fragile part is the equivalence label, not the sign. The frozen D2+H3 interval contains zero and crosses the margin, so this analysis does not establish the category for the neighbourhood; the X7-SC interval excludes zero and lies inside the margin, but that family mostly repeats D2+H3 (below). In relative terms, under D2+H3, the attacker's pooled mean gain against C$^*$ exceeds that against R3 by a fraction of \site{r_rel}. The two Q1 families are close to one test: X7-SC's search starts from the embedded D2+H3 winner, and in \site{r_x7_same} of the \site{r_x7_cells} Q1 cells it selected that winner, with identical evaluated gains; only fresh3 against C$^*$ and fresh4 against R3 differ. With the headline market, the same holds in \site{r_x7_all} of \site{r_x7_all_n} market--rule cells, which is why most of prediction Q3-a's differences are exactly zero. With each rule's strongest training-selected family instead (Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope}), the pooled difference is \site{r_env_pool}, and \site{r_env_f1} on fresh1, where X5-AG/v2 is C$^*$'s strongest family. C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain than C$^*$ (Section~\ref{sec:rules}); a comparator selected on attacker gain could change the direction label. The two rules differ more in whom they burden than in the attacker's gain. In the registered descriptive honest-replacement diagnostic at the headline market (Table~\ref{tab:ph-honest}), an honest provider replaces its identity at round \const{50}: R3 audits \site{r_hon_r3_0} and \site{r_hon_r3_1} of the replacement's served jobs in its first two ten-round age bins, C$^*$ audits \site{r_hon_cs_0} and \site{r_hon_cs_1}, and B3 about \site{r_hon_b3_0}. Under R3 the replacement earns less per round (relative to C$^*$, a fraction of \site{r_hon_rel}; the registered diagnostic stores bin means, so no interval is available) and loses more receipts to refunds paid to buyers on false audit failures; R3 directs more of the fixed audit capacity to this provider, which does not show higher total audit spending. Both decisions are about the prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test uses them through its references (addendum); their nominal error guarantees are not established on the fresh, selected-rule distributions (Section~\ref{sec:design}). The paired descriptive differences in Table~\ref{tab:q1-side} are small for efficiency, audit spending, refunds on false audit failures, and the market service fraction; the reference-profit column is in raw profit units. Splitting $d$ with the same normalisation into the attacked-profit term and the reference-profit term (Table~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, exploratory) shows that almost all of it is attacked profit: the attacker earns more under C$^*$ when it deviates, not when it is truthful.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:240:\paragraph{Which parts of R3 matter (Q2).} Under separately searched D2+H3 attacks, pooled over the fresh markets, the main effects on the gain are \site{r_q2_p} for the priority phase P and \site{r_q2_g} for newest-first residual targeting G, both reductions, and \site{r_q2_s} for pacing S, a small increase (X7-SC: \site{r_q2_s_x}). The two targeting parts are substitutes: the PG factorial contrast, half the difference between G's conditional effects, is \site{r_q2_pg}; G changes the gain by \site{r_q2_g_p0} without the priority phase and by only \site{r_q2_g_p1} with it, and the priority phase changes it by only \site{r_q2_p_g1} once G is in place (averaged over both pacing settings). R3's own comparison, $F_{111}$ minus $F_{011}$ (exploratory, from the evaluation records), is \site{r_dp} pooled: exactly zero on four markets, where both rules leave the attacker the same gain, and nonzero on fresh1 (\site{r_dp_f1}, larger than the doubling threshold, so the priority phase matters there) and fresh3. Given newest-first targeting the priority phase adds little at these audit ratios and capacities; at a lower audit ratio or a larger attacker capacity, half of a round's audits may no longer cover one provider's jobs, and the substitution need not hold. In the pooled contrasts, either P or G alone accounts for most of the measured reduction; adding the other contributes little. The pattern also appears in the fixed-attack contrasts averaged over the eight selected attacks (P: \site{r_q2_fixed_p}). Our registered conjectures (P negligible, the targeting parts additive, S helpful) failed as scored (Table~\ref{tab:predictions}); the stricter eligibility reading of Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} leaves them not assessed, with the estimates unchanged. X7-SC agrees (Table~\ref{tab:q2-x7sc}).
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:257:\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope: the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. These are finite-search results, not bounds on the families. Against B3 the selected envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it is \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. H4 was written in a separate session against the frozen defenders and committed by its hash before the lock; its author's containment note states that it contains D2+H3 exactly as a special case, so it extends the public family rather than offering a disjoint attack concept. Evaluated at the headline market only, its selected search gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and so is R3's robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness. At the headline market, the selected W0 attacks (no profit constraint) yield gross efficiency \site{r_q3_w0_f000} for B3 and \site{r_q3_w0} for R3, with gains relative to the truthful reference of \site{r_q3_w0g_f000} and \site{r_q3_w0g_f111}; C$^*$ is like R3. Under the prescribed W1 searches (mean gain at least zero), the selected feasible attacks yield efficiency \site{r_q3_w1} for R3, while B3 stays at \site{r_q3_w1_f000} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). R3's selected W1 policy has an estimated gain of exactly zero, and the doubled W1 searches for R3 and C$^*$ select truthful delivery (zero shading), with the option to replace after a failed audit left on: the W1 search found no profitable welfare attack against them. These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class. An episode-level mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode, has the probability-weighted mean of its components' gains and efficiencies, so it satisfies a mean-gain constraint only when those weighted gains do; the following rows are that arithmetic on the existing evaluation records. With the H4 attack as companion, at W0 weights selected on the evaluation records, the mixtures reach gross efficiencies of \site{r_ph_e_r3} for R3 and \site{r_ph_e_cs} for C$^*$ at non-negative estimated gain; with public D2+H3 companions, R3's mixture (\site{r_ph_e_r3_d}) meets the registered training constraint, and C$^*$'s does so at the smaller weight \site{r_ph_fw_cs}, with efficiency \site{r_ph_fe_cs} against \site{r_ph_w1_cs} under the registered W1 search (Tables~\ref{tab:ph-welfare} and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens}). Weights and companions were chosen on the evaluation seeds with an unadjusted nominal one-sided \const{95} percent bound, so these rows show that the prescribed W1 search did not consider episode-level mixtures that reach lower empirical efficiency; they are not out-of-sample estimates, and population profitability remains unestablished. The mixture efficiencies stay above \const{0.90}; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised members, the selected total gains are \site{r_q3_coal_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c2} against R3 (search flagged); for five, \site{r_q3_coal_f000_c5} and \site{r_q3_coal_f111} (total objective), while the policy selected for the minimum-member objective leaves R3's worst member a mean gain of \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c5min}; the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives \site{r_q3_db_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_db_f111} against R3. These comparisons establish neither coalition stability nor an ordering across every coalition family (Table~\ref{tab:q3-coalitions}). At $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves \site{r_rep_f111} under D2+H3; the repair R4 lowers this to \site{r_rep_r4} and C$^*$ with the capped schedule (C$^*$/S2) to \site{r_rep_cs2} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-repair}).
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:291:\paragraph{Search quality.} No cell is missing. \site{r_flag} of the \site{r_flag_n} doubling checks are flagged, and fresh-market factorial cells outside Q1 and Q4 have no doubling check, so their narrow intervals describe seed noise only. A nesting test on the existing records (exploratory) shows that unflagged cells can also be under-searched: X7-SC contains D2+H3, yet in \site{r_nest_over} of \site{r_nest_n} cells the X7-SC winner's evaluated gain falls short of the better of the frozen and doubled D2+H3 winners on the same seeds by more than the doubling threshold (at most \site{r_nest_max}). The report lists the search-flagged cells, and the addendum traces the inherited flags of every Q1 and Q4 decision through the records' references. Q2 and Q3 statements are descriptive; the cells they use carry the flags listed in the report. Table~\ref{tab:ph-stability} sets the Q1 estimates beside their re-evaluation with the registered configurations, and Table~\ref{tab:ph-q4-stability} compares the Q4 estimates, calibrated lower bounds, and decisions under both configurations and gives the adjusted market $p$-value under the registered configuration; the largest Q4 change is on fresh4, where the gain difference moves from \site{r_st_f4_old} to \site{r_st_f4_new}, still above the registered \const{0.10}.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:325:\paragraph{What the two tests support together.} At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, with R3's registered efficiency condition met. The gain is measured against each rule's frozen truthful reference; it does not isolate the marginal benefit of being allowed to replace identities, which would need a matched comparison with replacement disabled. The registered comparison does not establish that margin across all six sampled fresh markets. The pooled factorial contrasts and the comparison with C$^*$ support a measured benefit shared by more than one targeting design; they do not establish the same ordering for every variant on every market. At $\rho = \const{0.2}$, pacing slightly raises the measured gain while changing efficiency little; at $\rho = \const{1}$, the terminal reserve that pacing adds exhausts the allowance and causes R3's collapse, which the capped schedule only partly repairs.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:331:\paragraph{Limits.} The benchmark is a unilateral deviation against honest opponents; we claim neither equilibrium nor incentive compatibility. At the first-test parameters, Proposition~\ref{prop:terminal} establishes a profitable small same-offer shading deviation in a round with zero continuation loss under every studied rule. Exposure is bounded by the searched families and the optimiser. The fresh markets come from one stated neighbourhood, and the headline market was used in development. The honest opponents never replace their identities, so after the start a new identity marks a provider that has replaced; population churn of honest identities is untested. With such churn, newest-first targeting would spend audits on honest newcomers and could lose much of its targeting power, and the registered honest-replacement diagnostic covers a single replacement only. The comparator was selected on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:335:For the uniform-audit baselines B2 and B3 with refund-and-ban enforcement and no bonds, against honest rivals whose play does not depend on the focal identity, free replacement removes the ban's continuation cost. At the fixed parameters, the prescribed D2+H3 searches yield a lower measured gain for R3 than for B3 on every sampled market, but the registered gain-and-efficiency comparison passes on only three of six fresh markets, all three qualified by search flags. R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator are pooled-equivalent within the pre-set margin in the six sampled markets under both Q1 families, which are close to one test; across markets the difference varies in sign, and this analysis does not establish the ordering for the neighbourhood. At the headline market, the selected unconstrained welfare attacks yield gross efficiency of about \site{c_w0} against all three core rules and earn less than the truthful reference against R3 and C$^*$. The registered W1 search found no profitable welfare attack (gross efficiency near \site{c_w1}); exploratory mixtures of a welfare attack and a profitable attack reach lower efficiency at non-negative estimated gain, so it supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies. These conclusions concern frozen searches and references, with interval calibration admitted on development fixtures. The record of both tests, including the original failure, the amendments, the deviations, and the failed predictions, will be released with the paper.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:347:\caption{Development comparator selection (before the lock; development records): each credibility configuration's selected D2+H3 attack gain and gross efficiency at the inner search budget, and the two configurations rechecked at full budget, at the headline market and dev0, with R3's pacing and the fixed pair. Score: the development objective, the minimum over markets and families of the smaller of an efficiency margin and a gain margin, with the market and family that decide it; the efficiency margin was the smaller at every configuration.}\label{tab:comparator-dev}
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:351:\paragraph{Lemma~\ref{lem:local}.} Shading saves $sg$. Each audited escrow is refunded with probability $d$ instead of $d_0$. Given $K$, independence of delivery noise gives the probabilities $1 - (1-d)^K$ and $1 - (1-d_0)^K$ of at least one failure; the continuation loss is incurred once. Taking expectations over $U$ gives the identity.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:353:\paragraph{Corollary~\ref{cor:uniform}.} Let $V_{\mathrm{clean}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{fail}}$ be the best continuation values after no focal failure and after at least one, so $C_U = V_{\mathrm{clean}} - V_{\mathrm{fail}}$. By the first condition, immediate free replacement attains the clean distribution of future states after a failure, so $V_{\mathrm{fail}} \ge V_{\mathrm{clean}}$. By the second, any plan after a failure (keeping the banned identity and waiting, or replacing at any later time) can be copied after no failure, so $V_{\mathrm{fail}} \le V_{\mathrm{clean}}$. Hence $C_U = 0$. For B2 and B3 the second condition holds because a failed identity carries only its ban, with no priority or payment privilege, a clean provider can copy waiting by not offering and can itself replace its identity, no bond is lost, and the audit budget and rival states do not depend on the focal identity's history; replacement restores eligibility before the next allocation. Refunds of the failed jobs in the current round still enter Lemma~\ref{lem:local}. A rule that gave priority to an identity that waits out its ban would violate the second condition, and the corollary would not apply to it.
**Follow-through**

“Resolved” means that the stated concern has been answered. It does not certify unavailable implementation or provenance evidence.

| Previous item | Status | Revised sentence and page | Assessment |
|---|---|---|---|
| **1. Equivalence, margin, markets** | **Partly resolved** | “The D2+H3 interval contains zero and crosses the margin, so this analysis does not establish the category for the neighbourhood; the X7-SC interval excludes zero and lies inside the margin, but that family mostly repeats D2+H3 (below).” **p. 13** | The new interval and relative difference address the precision problem. Keeping the registered category is legitimate. Its economic materiality remains a convention rather than an established indifference threshold. Search uncertainty remains. |
| **2. Two largely overlapping families** | **Resolved as disclosure** | “The two Q1 families are close to one test: X7-SC’s search starts from the embedded D2+H3 winner, and in 10 of the 12 Q1 cells it selected that winner, with identical evaluated gains; only fresh3 against C∗ and fresh4 against R3 differ.” **p. 13** | Clear and appropriately prominent. Aggregate equality counts reproduce. Search-source provenance does not independently reproduce from this packet. |
| **3. Weak searches** | **Partly resolved** | “21 of the 98 doubling checks are flagged, and fresh-market factorial cells outside Q1 and Q4 have no doubling check, so their narrow intervals describe seed noise only.” **p. 16** | The disclosure and envelope calculation are correct. They expose the problem; they do not close the remaining search gap. |
| **4. No honest churn** | **Not resolved empirically** | “With such churn, newest-first targeting would spend audits on honest newcomers and could lose much of its targeting power, and the registered honest-replacement diagnostic covers a single replacement only.” **p. 25** | The limitation is now explicit. A single honest replacement in an otherwise honest population does not measure competition between honest newcomers and an adapting attacker. |
| **5. Comparator selected on efficiency** | **Partly resolved** | “The comparator was selected on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain.” **p. 25** | The correction is useful. Table 24 reveals the screening weakness, but its complete calculation is unavailable here. No fresh-market runner-up result is supplied. |
| **6. Corollary scope** | **Resolved mathematically; implementation check limited** | “By the second, any plan after a failure (keeping the banned identity and waiting, or replacing at any later time) can be copied after no failure, so Vfail ≤ Vclean.” **p. 27** | The added second condition closes the missing inequality. The statement now specifies the relevant rival behaviour and re-entry timing. B2’s implementation remains unavailable. |
| **7. Lemma wording and unused theorem** | **Resolved in substance** | “An identity’s age is the number of rounds since its first offer.” **p. 4** “The lemma bounds the audits a replacement receives, not its deterrence, and the bound can be zero (ties, no served jobs, newer entrants, or a quota already spent).” **p. 2** | “Audit dues” is gone; age, overload, and the coverage interpretation are clear. Theorem 1 is explicitly unused. Moving it is an editorial choice. |
| **8. Mixture contribution and degenerate W1 result** | **Resolved in substance** | “R3’s selected W1 policy has an estimated gain of exactly zero, and the doubled W1 searches for R3 and C∗ select truthful delivery (zero shading), with the option to replace after a failed audit left on: the W1 search found no profitable welfare attack against them.” **p. 15** | The null result and mixture arithmetic are now disclosed. The mixture no longer leads the abstract or contribution list. “Supported” in Table 16 still needs careful reading as the registered nonnegative-gain classification. |
| **9. Priority phase redundancy** | **Partly resolved** | “Second, newest-first targeting does almost all the work: once it is in place, R3’s priority window changes the attacker’s gain by only −0.001.” **p. 1** | The finding is now prominent, but this sentence assigns a pacing-averaged D2+H3 effect to R3 specifically. See the new problems below. |
| **10. Statistics versus main uncertainty** | **Partly resolved** | “The registered intervals measure seed uncertainty in six fixed markets.” **pp. 12–13** | Correct distinction. The market intervals improve interpretation. They do not include repeated-search uncertainty or establish coverage for the selected-rule distributions. Calibration still precedes the substantive results. |
| **11. Process and prediction disclosures** | **Resolved as disclosure** | “The predictions differ in stakes and are not independent (Q1-a, its alternative, and Q1-d are decided by the same Q1 categories, and Q3-e passes by less than one hundred-thousandth), so the count of fulfilled predictions is not a validation score.” **p. 17** | The H4 session and executor role are disclosed on p. 10; containment and headline-only evaluation appear on pp. 14–15. The prediction qualification is correct. I cannot audit session custody from the ledger alone. |
| **Related work** | **Resolved for the requested additions** | “Reputation mechanisms with pure moral hazard and the cost of changing identities are analysed in [9].” **p. 3** | Harrington, Dellarocas, and Golle–Mironov are now included as references **18, 9, and 17**, respectively, on pp. 28–29. `RESPONSE.md` reports Crossref checks. I did not independently verify those checks because external reading was prohibited. Addition does not establish novelty. |
| **Presentation** | **Partly resolved** | “Three findings lead.” **p. 2** | The contribution list is clearer. The paper still devotes extensive main-text space to process, scoring, and secondary results. Main results begin on p. 12; the conclusion is on p. 26. The venue version remains unfinished. |

**New-number checks**

Here, “reproduced” means recalculated from the supplied numerical inputs. It does not mean that I reran the simulator.

For the market intervals, I used  
\[
\bar d\pm t_{0.975,5}s_d/\sqrt6.
\]
Frozen values come from `stability-result.json → cells → mean_old`. Doubled values come from the per-seed `heldout.gain` arrays embedded in `report.md`.

